{"name":"Balsa Stone","hex":"#cbbb92","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"balsa","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large tree, Ochroma pyramidale, native to tropical America, with wood that is very light in weight.","The wood of this tree."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/balsa"},{"term":"stone","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard earthen substance that can form rocks; especially, such substance when regarded as a building material.","A piece of such material: a block of stone, rock or a pebble."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Constructed of stone.","Having the appearance of stone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ochroma","blocks":[{"text":"Ochroma pyramidale, commonly known as balsa, is a large, fast-growing tree native to the Americas. It is the sole member of the genus Ochroma, and is classified in the subfamily Bombacoideae of the mallow family Malvaceae. The tree is famous for its wide usage in woodworking, due to its softness and its high strength compared to its low density. The name balsa is the Spanish word for \"raft\" and the Portuguese word for ferry."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Balso_%28Ochroma_pyramidale%29_%2814723405443%29.jpg/330px-Balso_%28Ochroma_pyramidale%29_%2814723405443%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ochroma"},{"title":"Rock (geology)","blocks":[{"text":"In geology, a rock is any naturally occurring solid mass or aggregate of minerals or mineraloid matter. It is categorized by the minerals included, its chemical composition, and the way in which it is formed. Rocks form the Earth's outer solid layer, the crust, and most of its interior, except for the liquid outer core and pockets of magma in the asthenosphere. The study of rocks involves multiple subdisciplines of geology, including petrology and mineralogy. It may be limited to rocks found on Earth, or it may include planetary geology that studies the rocks of other celestial objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg/330px-Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_(geology)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Ochroma_pyramidalis_MS_3425.jpg/500px-Ochroma_pyramidalis_MS_3425.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Ochroma_pyramidalis_MS_3425.jpg/1280px-Ochroma_pyramidalis_MS_3425.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Ochroma_pyramidalis_MS_3425.jpg/500px-Ochroma_pyramidalis_MS_3425.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Balsa in Limbe Botanical Garden, Cameroon","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOchroma_pyramidalis_MS_3425.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Balsa.jpg/330px-Balsa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Balsa.jpg/330px-Balsa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Three different sizes of balsa wood stock","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABalsa.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Ochroma_pyramidale1FrancesWHorne.jpg/120px-Ochroma_pyramidale1FrancesWHorne.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Ochroma_pyramidale1FrancesWHorne.jpg/250px-Ochroma_pyramidale1FrancesWHorne.jpg?utm_source